WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.199 --> 00:00:04.979
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Dr. Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.979 --> 00:00:10.440
Today we are going to see if a robot can learn
to play table tennis.

00:00:10.440 --> 00:00:15.209
Spoiler alert, the answer is yes, quite well
in fact.

00:00:15.209 --> 00:00:21.510
That is surprising, but what is even more
surprising is how quickly it learned to do

00:00:21.510 --> 00:00:22.510
that.

00:00:22.510 --> 00:00:27.790
Recently, we have seen a growing number of
techniques where robots learn in a computer

00:00:27.790 --> 00:00:32.529
simulation, and then, get deployed into the
real world.

00:00:32.529 --> 00:00:35.350
Yes, that sounds like science fiction.

00:00:35.350 --> 00:00:38.060
So, does this work in practice?

00:00:38.060 --> 00:00:43.500
Well, I’ll give you two recent examples,
and you can decide for yourself.

00:00:43.500 --> 00:00:49.380
What you see here is example number one, where
OpenAI’s robot hand that learned to dexterously

00:00:49.380 --> 00:00:53.220
rotate this Rubik cube to a given target state.

00:00:53.220 --> 00:00:54.470
How did it do it?

00:00:54.470 --> 00:00:58.400
Yes, you guessed it right, it learned in a
simulation.

00:00:58.400 --> 00:01:04.719
However, no simulation is as detailed as the
real world, so they used a technique called

00:01:04.719 --> 00:01:10.799
automatic domain randomization in which they
create a large number of random environments,

00:01:10.799 --> 00:01:17.289
each of which are a little different, and
the AI is meant to learn how to solve many

00:01:17.289 --> 00:01:20.459
different variants of the same problem.

00:01:20.459 --> 00:01:21.740
And the result?

00:01:21.740 --> 00:01:24.140
Did it learn general knowledge from that?

00:01:24.140 --> 00:01:30.170
Yes, what’s more, this became not only a
dexterous robot hand that can execute these

00:01:30.170 --> 00:01:37.210
rotations, but, we can make up creative ways
to torment this little machine, and it still

00:01:37.210 --> 00:01:38.729
stood its ground.

00:01:38.729 --> 00:01:45.859
Okay, so this works, but is this concept good
enough for commercial applications?

00:01:45.859 --> 00:01:46.859
You bet.

00:01:46.859 --> 00:01:53.829
Example number two, Tesla uses no less than
a simulated game world to train their self-driving

00:01:53.829 --> 00:01:54.829
cars.

00:01:54.829 --> 00:02:00.329
For instance, when we are in this synthetic
video game, it is suddenly much easier to

00:02:00.329 --> 00:02:02.759
teach the algorithm safely.

00:02:02.759 --> 00:02:10.399
You can also make any scenario easier, harder,
replace a car with a dog, or a pack of dogs,

00:02:10.399 --> 00:02:16.480
and make many similar examples so that the
AI can learn from these “what if” situations

00:02:16.480 --> 00:02:18.540
as much as possible.

00:02:18.540 --> 00:02:25.379
Now, that’s all great, but today, we are
going to see whether this concept can be generalized

00:02:25.379 --> 00:02:28.290
to playing table tennis.

00:02:28.290 --> 00:02:34.040
And I have to be honest…I am very enthused,
but a little skeptical too.

00:02:34.040 --> 00:02:40.409
This task requires finesse, rapid movement,
and predicting what is about to happen in

00:02:40.409 --> 00:02:41.890
the near future.

00:02:41.890 --> 00:02:44.410
It really is the whole package, isn’t it.

00:02:44.410 --> 00:02:49.129
Now, let’s enter the training simulation
and see how this goes.

00:02:49.129 --> 00:02:56.189
First, we hit the ball over to its side, specify
a desired return position, and ask it to practice

00:02:56.189 --> 00:02:59.380
returning the ball around this desired position.

00:02:59.380 --> 00:03:04.950
Then, after a quick retraining step against
the ball throwing machine, we observe the

00:03:04.950 --> 00:03:06.799
first amazing thing.

00:03:06.799 --> 00:03:13.079
You see, the first cool thing here is that
it practices against side spin and top spin

00:03:13.079 --> 00:03:14.079
balls.

00:03:14.079 --> 00:03:15.359
What are those?

00:03:15.359 --> 00:03:19.950
These are techniques where the players hit
the ball in ways to make their trajectory

00:03:19.950 --> 00:03:22.430
much more difficult to predict.

00:03:22.430 --> 00:03:28.040
Okay, enough of this, now, hold on to your
papers, and let’s see how the final version

00:03:28.040 --> 00:03:33.269
of the AI fares against a player.

00:03:33.269 --> 00:03:34.730
And…whoa.

00:03:34.730 --> 00:03:37.909
It really made the transition into the real
world.

00:03:37.909 --> 00:03:42.040
Look at that, this seems like it could go
on forever.

00:03:42.040 --> 00:03:45.670
Let’s watch for a few seconds.

00:03:45.670 --> 00:03:47.579
Yep, still going.

00:03:47.579 --> 00:03:49.540
Still going.

00:03:49.540 --> 00:03:51.920
But, we are not done yet.

00:03:51.920 --> 00:03:52.920
Not even close!

00:03:52.920 --> 00:03:57.230
We said at the start of the video that this
training is quick.

00:03:57.230 --> 00:03:58.230
How quick?

00:03:58.230 --> 00:04:04.050
Well, if you have been holding on to your
papers, now, squeeze that paper, because all

00:04:04.050 --> 00:04:08.870
the robot took was 1.5 hours of training.

00:04:08.870 --> 00:04:12.400
And wait, there are two more mind-blowing
numbers here.

00:04:12.400 --> 00:04:16.410
It can return 98% of the balls.

00:04:16.410 --> 00:04:23.370
And most of them are within 25 centimeters,
or about 10 inches of the desired spot.

00:04:23.370 --> 00:04:30.750
And again, great news, this is one more technique
that does not require Google or OpenAI-level

00:04:30.750 --> 00:04:34.290
resources to make something really amazing.

00:04:34.290 --> 00:04:35.660
Loving it.

00:04:35.660 --> 00:04:42.400
And you know, this is the way to make an excellent
excuse to play table tennis during work hours.

00:04:42.400 --> 00:04:44.200
They really made it work.

00:04:44.200 --> 00:04:46.350
Huge congratulations to the team.

00:04:46.350 --> 00:04:49.530
Now, of course, not even this technique is
perfect.

00:04:49.530 --> 00:04:55.870
We noted that it can handle side spin and
top spin balls, but can’t deal with backspin

00:04:55.870 --> 00:05:04.190
balls yet, because, get this, quoting ”it
causes too much acceleration in a robot joint”.

00:05:04.190 --> 00:05:07.060
Yes - a robot with joint pain.

00:05:07.060 --> 00:05:08.460
What a time to be alive!

00:05:08.460 --> 00:05:10.480
Now, one more thing.

00:05:10.480 --> 00:05:16.010
As of the making of this video, this was seen
by a grand total of… 54 people.

00:05:16.010 --> 00:05:21.951
Again, there is a real possibility that if
we don’t talk about this amazing work, no

00:05:21.951 --> 00:05:23.060
one will.

00:05:23.060 --> 00:05:26.060
And this is why I started Two Minute Papers.

00:05:26.060 --> 00:05:29.280
Thank you very much for coming on this journey
with me.

00:05:29.280 --> 00:06:13.980
Please subscribe if you wish to see more 

00:06:13.980 --> 00:06:22.290
of these.

00:06:22.290 --> 00:06:25.240
Thanks for watching and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

